WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:04.210
Speaker 1: It's great to welcome back to the
program today, Grace Blakely, who is a staff

00:00:04.210 --> 00:00:10.490
writer at Tribune magazine and also author
of the book Stolen How to Save the World from

00:00:10.490 --> 00:00:12.360
Financialization A Grace.

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:13.790
It's great to have you back on today.

00:00:13.790 --> 00:00:15.130
Speaker 3: Great to be here.

00:00:15.130 --> 00:00:16.130
Thanks for having me.

00:00:16.130 --> 00:00:21.240
Speaker 1: So, OK, let's I want to talk about
whether there will really be any longer term

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:24.369
changes to the economy into the world of work
from the pandemic.

00:00:24.369 --> 00:00:29.140
You know, we just kind of generally have this
understanding that things that seem like,

00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:34.970
oh, we're never going back, humans forget,
humans re-adapt and things sort of return

00:00:34.970 --> 00:00:37.930
to the mean in many cases.

00:00:37.930 --> 00:00:43.720
Are there aspects of work and or the relationship
of people to to the economy, particularly

00:00:43.720 --> 00:00:47.140
in Western economies, that you think will
be permanently altered as a result of the

00:00:47.140 --> 00:00:48.140
pandemic?

00:00:48.140 --> 00:00:52.710
Speaker 3: Well, I think as with any crisis,
what happens next is really going to depend

00:00:52.710 --> 00:00:57.940
on what we demand and and how we kind of choose
to reorganize society when we when we emerge

00:00:57.940 --> 00:00:59.150
from it.

00:00:59.150 --> 00:01:04.330
I don't think that we will see a kind of return
to normal, whatever normal was, because, I

00:01:04.330 --> 00:01:09.760
mean, even before the pandemic, we had a lot
of quite deep problems with the global economy,

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:14.069
everything from this kind of long term stagnation
in productivity and investment through to

00:01:14.069 --> 00:01:19.759
the stagnation in wages, through to the kind
of massive debt crisis that that we were going

00:01:19.759 --> 00:01:20.759
through in the US.

00:01:20.759 --> 00:01:23.579
People were talking about a corporate debt
bubble.

00:01:23.579 --> 00:01:27.380
And even before the pandemic started, which,
of course, fed into a lot of the problems

00:01:27.380 --> 00:01:29.329
that we saw at the start.

00:01:29.329 --> 00:01:32.830
So there were already all these kind of deep
seeded issues that I raised by my my thoughts.

00:01:32.830 --> 00:01:34.170
But imagine that stolen.

00:01:34.170 --> 00:01:40.680
So obviously going back to that kind of normal
would not be desirable.

00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:46.840
We also then had some kind of more, I suppose,
longer term structural problems to deal with.

00:01:46.840 --> 00:01:51.799
So things like, you know, obviously climate
breakdown, global inequality, inequality within

00:01:51.799 --> 00:01:53.380
nations as well.

00:01:53.380 --> 00:01:56.259
And some of those things have gotten worse
over the course of the pandemic.

00:01:56.259 --> 00:02:02.240
So if we look at inequality, for example,
globally, we've seen this big divergence in

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:07.179
outcomes between wealthier states that have
been able to tackle the pandemic, have been

00:02:07.179 --> 00:02:13.540
able to have a big stimulus programs, big
programs to support workers and businesses

00:02:13.540 --> 00:02:19.140
and states in the global south, which have
suffered from the largest outflow of capital

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:23.660
ever recorded as a result of the pandemic
and are struggling just to be able to kind

00:02:23.660 --> 00:02:28.910
of provide the basic services that the populations
need, let alone put in place the measures

00:02:28.910 --> 00:02:30.590
that are going to allow them to recover.

00:02:30.590 --> 00:02:33.960
And actually a lot of them are kind of on
the verge of defaulting on their debts.

00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:36.700
And then, you know, they're not able to access
the vaccine either.

00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:39.770
So that divergence is going to be really,
really problematic.

00:02:39.770 --> 00:02:41.240
Inequality within countries.

00:02:41.240 --> 00:02:46.000
We've seen this big divide between people
who've been able to amass savings and who

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:49.490
will come out of the pandemic, go out and
spend all that money potentially driving a

00:02:49.490 --> 00:02:55.470
bit of inflation and everyone else who maybe
has actually seen that debt increase and is

00:02:55.470 --> 00:02:56.770
struggling to make ends meet.

00:02:56.770 --> 00:02:58.940
So that divide is going to increase as well.

00:02:58.940 --> 00:03:04.110
And really what happens next is all going
to be shaped by policy, by, you know, whether

00:03:04.110 --> 00:03:09.290
or not we have a kind of just recovery or
whether we see the same sorts of policies

00:03:09.290 --> 00:03:13.440
as what we saw after the financial crisis,
where the costs of that crisis were largely

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:18.170
borne by working people
Speaker 1: to pick one micro issue and see

00:03:18.170 --> 00:03:21.780
whether you think it's really relevant to
the bigger picture or it's just something

00:03:21.780 --> 00:03:24.820
people are liking to write articles about.

00:03:24.820 --> 00:03:27.400
There's this idea that, OK, take Manhattan.

00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:34.440
There's all this empty office space in Manhattan
because of work from home and small businesses

00:03:34.440 --> 00:03:38.890
moving out and big businesses will reduce
their footprint and just reduce the amount

00:03:38.890 --> 00:03:40.410
of square footage.

00:03:40.410 --> 00:03:44.970
And then you read an analysis which takes
a sort of conservative view on it, which is,

00:03:44.970 --> 00:03:50.340
yeah, you know, rents will go down a few percent
in the immediate, but it's still Manhattan.

00:03:50.340 --> 00:03:51.520
It's still really expensive.

00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:55.750
And then three or four years from now, if
there's not another pandemic, they'll probably

00:03:55.750 --> 00:03:58.910
sort of be back to being close to where they
were.

00:03:58.910 --> 00:04:02.980
And some of the big offices will be replaced
with coworking spaces.

00:04:02.980 --> 00:04:08.490
And it won't really change the big picture
in a serious way, something like that.

00:04:08.490 --> 00:04:13.800
Do you think that that even ranks when we
think about, like the systemic changes or

00:04:13.800 --> 00:04:17.920
is the analysis that it's a couple percentage
points and it won't really make a difference

00:04:17.920 --> 00:04:19.010
is the correct analysis.

00:04:19.010 --> 00:04:26.520
Speaker 3: I mean, the big driver of like
real estate prices at the moment is not necessarily

00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:27.520
demand.

00:04:27.520 --> 00:04:30.139
It's more speculation, particularly in big
cities.

00:04:30.139 --> 00:04:36.680
So in places like Manhattan and London, there
has not been the kind of fall in in rents

00:04:36.680 --> 00:04:41.980
and particularly in actual house prices that
you would assume would happen as people leave

00:04:41.980 --> 00:04:45.950
the capitals when London, for example, the
population has fallen, according to some estimates,

00:04:45.950 --> 00:04:50.380
by around seven hundred thousand since the
pandemic started, which is huge.

00:04:50.380 --> 00:04:56.200
And that hasn't really been too much of an
impact on rents or or real estate prices.

00:04:56.200 --> 00:04:59.530
It's different in different parts of the market,
but broadly speaking, things haven't changed

00:04:59.530 --> 00:05:00.530
dramatically.

00:05:00.530 --> 00:05:05.720
And of course, the main reason for that is
quantitative easing is this policy adopted

00:05:05.720 --> 00:05:10.070
by central banks of creating new money to
purchase assets from private investors, which

00:05:10.070 --> 00:05:14.460
is pumping tons and tons and tons of cash
into the financial system.

00:05:14.460 --> 00:05:18.650
I think the last time I checked, it was about
nine trillion dollars worth of new money had

00:05:18.650 --> 00:05:22.620
been created predominately by the big four
central banks to try and prop up the financial

00:05:22.620 --> 00:05:25.860
system and actually to try and keep asset
prices high.

00:05:25.860 --> 00:05:27.760
This is what central banks are trying to do
at the moment.

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:29.169
They're trying to keep equities high.

00:05:29.169 --> 00:05:33.830
They're trying to keep real estate prices
high, basically, because if those prices were

00:05:33.830 --> 00:05:39.480
to fall, that could create a wider financial
crisis, basically because, you know, some

00:05:39.480 --> 00:05:41.669
of that is used as collateral for lending.

00:05:41.669 --> 00:05:46.470
And if you start to see that fall, you got
kind of debt deflation cycle of the kind that

00:05:46.470 --> 00:05:48.310
we saw during the financial crisis.

00:05:48.310 --> 00:05:53.910
So policy makers and central bankers are actually
going for high asset prices or at least stable

00:05:53.910 --> 00:05:58.010
asset prices, and this model of quantitative
easing is how they're doing it.

00:05:58.010 --> 00:06:01.780
And that's likely to be with us for quite
some time now.

00:06:01.780 --> 00:06:06.120
A lot of it is going to depend on what happens
in the rest of the economy to do with inflation

00:06:06.120 --> 00:06:07.670
and whatever.

00:06:07.670 --> 00:06:11.530
But broadly speaking, central bankers know
that the big thing driving asset prices at

00:06:11.530 --> 00:06:13.910
the moment is their policies.

00:06:13.910 --> 00:06:18.110
And if those policies stop, you could see
quite significant fall in asset prices, which

00:06:18.110 --> 00:06:22.310
would have kind of negative implications in
terms of financial stability, but which would

00:06:22.310 --> 00:06:27.540
actually probably correct the massive wealth
inequality that we've seen really just go

00:06:27.540 --> 00:06:31.680
through the roof, because the pandemic people
like Jeff Bezos becoming extraordinarily wealthy,

00:06:31.680 --> 00:06:36.050
basically because money is being plowed into
Amazon shares because of central bank policy.

00:06:36.050 --> 00:06:39.550
The same thing is happening in with real estate
as well.

00:06:39.550 --> 00:06:43.910
So unless we see like a big shift in policy,
that probably isn't going to change.

00:06:43.910 --> 00:06:48.660
Then again, it is hard to see how the pattern
that we've seen over the last 40 years and

00:06:48.660 --> 00:06:54.220
just constantly increasing house prices can
continue forever.

00:06:54.220 --> 00:07:00.340
So it's a real question as to the interaction
between policy and whether or not we see another

00:07:00.340 --> 00:07:02.610
shock that does actually lead to some sort
of correction.

00:07:02.610 --> 00:07:06.810
But I wouldn't put any money on house prices
properly falling in the kind of short to medium

00:07:06.810 --> 00:07:07.810
term.

00:07:07.810 --> 00:07:08.810
Speaker 1: Yes.

00:07:08.810 --> 00:07:12.100
Speaking of that, I'd be interested to hear
