00:11:08.330 --> 00:11:13.050
Obama's stimulus is going to push inflation
off the charts.

00:11:13.050 --> 00:11:15.730
Trump's first covid stimulus, second covid.

00:11:15.730 --> 00:11:20.910
And they were more or less wrong all along
and inflation has been quite, quite stable.

00:11:20.910 --> 00:11:26.000
We now have a situation finally where we're
starting to see some higher inflation numbers.

00:11:26.000 --> 00:11:30.510
Some of it is because we're comparing to the
second quarter of twenty twenty when there

00:11:30.510 --> 00:11:32.459
was a total demand collapse.

00:11:32.459 --> 00:11:33.980
So part of that may be an artifact of that.

00:11:33.980 --> 00:11:38.250
It's still sort of remains to be seen.

00:11:38.250 --> 00:11:39.610
How should we interpret that?

00:11:39.610 --> 00:11:44.500
Like is the idea of the inflationary spiral
when governments do stuff, something we can

00:11:44.500 --> 00:11:48.420
just put to bed at this point, or is there
anything to it?

00:11:48.420 --> 00:11:53.300
Speaker 3: People get very hysterical about
inflation, and this was kind of a proper neoliberal

00:11:53.300 --> 00:11:55.100
talking point during the 1980s.

00:11:55.100 --> 00:12:00.649
Yes, basically at that time inflation was
people were actually worried about inflation.

00:12:00.649 --> 00:12:04.150
I mean, they would frame it as being worried
about inflation because they could say, well,

00:12:04.150 --> 00:12:07.400
it's the price of the goods and services that
you're buying that's going up.

00:12:07.400 --> 00:12:10.769
Really, it was a mechanism to discipline organized
labor.

00:12:10.769 --> 00:12:15.830
So you saw well, for example, in the US with
the Volcker shock interest rates going through

00:12:15.830 --> 00:12:19.500
the roof, it creates a kind of worldwide economic
crisis.

00:12:19.500 --> 00:12:21.209
So a debt crisis in the global south.

00:12:21.209 --> 00:12:26.940
But it was also used as a tool to create mass
unemployment so that the labor unions could

00:12:26.940 --> 00:12:30.850
be broken and this kind of neoliberal model
could be set up.

00:12:30.850 --> 00:12:34.540
And since then, we haven't had the same kinds
of back then yet.

00:12:34.540 --> 00:12:35.780
They were kind of some issues with inflation.

00:12:35.780 --> 00:12:39.820
I mean, a lot of it was actually driven by
the oil price spike rather than anything else.

00:12:39.820 --> 00:12:42.410
But inflation hasn't really been too much
of an issue.

00:12:42.410 --> 00:12:44.709
And actually, since the financial crisis,
it's been too low.

00:12:44.709 --> 00:12:50.700
I mean, you can think about inflationary pressure
as something that happens when the output

00:12:50.700 --> 00:12:51.700
gap closes.

00:12:51.700 --> 00:12:55.870
So that's the gap between the amount of stuff
that we could be producing if every resource

00:12:55.870 --> 00:12:59.080
was being put to use and the amount of stuff
we're actually producing.

00:12:59.080 --> 00:13:00.080
Right.

00:13:00.080 --> 00:13:04.120
So when you get inflation, it's because you're
starting to get to the point where all the

00:13:04.120 --> 00:13:07.690
resources that exist in the economy are being
used to their greatest extent.

00:13:07.690 --> 00:13:08.690
Right.

00:13:08.690 --> 00:13:12.250
Or maybe more than those resources are being
used or getting some pressure on prices.

00:13:12.250 --> 00:13:15.820
But when that isn't happening, so, you know,
there's underemployment, underutilization

00:13:15.820 --> 00:13:17.930
of resources, you don't have inflationary
pressure.

00:13:17.930 --> 00:13:22.420
And that's what we were seeing because there
was this big shortage in demand that came

00:13:22.420 --> 00:13:26.210
from low levels of investment, low levels
of government spending and just general kind

00:13:26.210 --> 00:13:29.190
of a lack of optimism about what was going
on in the economy legitimately.

00:13:29.190 --> 00:13:35.670
So today what we're seeing is because it's
less about the fact that demand is going up,

00:13:35.670 --> 00:13:38.100
demand is going up and it will continue to
go up.

00:13:38.100 --> 00:13:42.960
It's more about the fact that the actual amount
that we could produce has contracted so much

00:13:42.960 --> 00:13:48.959
so that that kind of longer term supply side
of the economy has really become much more

00:13:48.959 --> 00:13:49.959
constrained.

00:13:49.959 --> 00:13:54.070
A big reason for that is what's going on in
the rest of the world.

00:13:54.070 --> 00:13:57.390
And this is why it's really, really important
for economies in the global north, aside from

00:13:57.390 --> 00:14:02.410
the kind of obvious moral reasons to pay attention
to the global economy.

00:14:02.410 --> 00:14:06.870
Because if you start to see, you know, say,
the Indian variant spreading throughout the

00:14:06.870 --> 00:14:11.899
global south, production shuts down, can't
get access to the commodities that you know,

00:14:11.899 --> 00:14:17.350
as massive importers, countries like the US
and the UK need just to kind of get by, then

00:14:17.350 --> 00:14:22.380
you will start seeing a huge amount of inflation,
especially since you have all these savings

00:14:22.380 --> 00:14:25.040
that middle class people have and they want
to go out and spend in the economy.

00:14:25.040 --> 00:14:29.370
So that's, I think, the biggest potential
pressure that we're going to be seeing inflation

00:14:29.370 --> 00:14:30.920
wise from now on.

00:14:30.920 --> 00:14:35.399
And ultimately, it's going to hurt working
class people more than it's going to hurt

00:14:35.399 --> 00:14:36.399
middle class people.

00:14:36.399 --> 00:14:37.400
Speaker 1: No question.

00:14:37.400 --> 00:14:38.680
No question about that.

00:14:38.680 --> 00:14:43.380
We've been speaking with Grace Blakely, staff
writer at Tribune magazine and also author

00:14:43.380 --> 00:14:46.530
of Stolen How to Save the World from Financialization.

00:14:46.530 --> 00:14:48.650
Grace, always great speaking with you.

00:14:48.650 --> 00:14:49.680
Speaker 3: And you, too.

00:14:49.680 --> 00:14:50.680
Thanks.

00:14:50.680 --> 00:14:50.681
David.

